Date: Wed, 08 Sep 1993 00:12:47 -0400
From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: PHIL: Ethics etc. 

Jay R. Freeman says:
> [Long thoughtful post by Ratha, which I infer was a response to
>  several of mine.]
> 
> I see no internal inconsistency in your position, however, your
> meta-definition, that "rights" must be reciprocally granted, is not
> the way I am used to using the word.

Its the way I am used to it.

Rights are a contractual notion. I agree not to shoot at you, and in
exchange you don't shoot at me. When one of us breaks the implicit
contract, thats when you lose your rights. On the other hand, a
squirrel cannot be induced to respect your rights -- and thus cannot
earn any of its own.

> We should be careful to specify which definition we are using should
> we have further discussions on the subject.

"Rights" in an absolute sense are a useless notion -- they are not
carved into the sky or anything. They are a social convention -- and
thus arise out of human interactions. They are a fiction that WE
create. The notion of ascribing rights to creatures that cannot
negotiate or understand is meaningless.

> My own position on the squirrel/architect confrontation would be that
> human development should make considerable effort to preserve quality
> habitat for substantial numbers of members of displaced species (with
> long discussions possible about the detailed meanings of several of
> those words), and perhaps that trees should not be cut down during
> nesting season unless one is either sure that no squirrels are displaced,
> or fosters any juveniles with some organization that will rehabilitate
> them.  Ditto for birds, etc.

Why should we care? I can see a reason to preserve the environment for
HUMAN purposes -- even I will admit that Mark Desilet's environs in
Boulder Creek are a lot more pleasant in many ways than downtown
Manhattan. However, this is an aesthetic choice made for the benefit
of humans -- not for the redwoods and squirrels -- and is purely
selfish, providing no reason at all other than personal desire for
natural surroundings not to blow the brains out of any passing animal
one feels like. Given this, I have trouble seeing why one should, per
se, minimally impact the environment. Presumably, the rule is "do what
is best for you", which may sometimes lead to preservation, and may at
other times lead to strip mining. Certainly squirrels have no "rights"
that I can see to be minimally disturbed.

Perry

