Subject: PHIL: Ethics etc.

F.E.S., I'm glad we could come to an understanding on definitions. What
confuses it is the double use of the word in English to mean both basic
rights (like the inalienable ones, as in the Dec of I) and
"entitlement", as in, "I have every right to ride this bus." My reason
for reiterating this rather dead point is to address something nitpicky,
brought up by Perry, in his much-appreciated defense of my last post: 

> (Quoting F.E.S.):
> > We should be careful to specify which definition we are using should
> > we have further discussions on the subject.
> 
> "Rights" in an absolute sense are a useless notion -- they are not
> carved into the sky or anything. They are a social convention -- and
> thus arise out of human interactions. They are a fiction that WE
> create. The notion of ascribing rights to creatures that cannot
> negotiate or understand is meaningless.

I just wanted say that rights, in their most basic sense, are not
completely fictional. While remaining our invention, (after all,
concepts are also our invention) there are, empirically and rationally,
certain "rights" that can be considered "universal" to all intelligent
beings; i.e., existence, property, freedom, etc. Why? Because said
beings will defend them. If you don't understand rights, you can't
defend them. That's the distinguishing criterion. Hence, you can
"recognize" rights (the social convention part), but cannot "attribute"
them arbitrarily, because they are dependent on the being's ability to
hold them. The squirrel can't and won't reason with you if you cut down
its tree, so it'll be pissed for a while and then sulk off to find
another tree. This is not really the same as "might makes right." It is
more like "ability makes right." Mental ability, that is. 

It is ludicrous to suggest that human progress should halt for a bunch
of little furry animals. If they could be reasoned with, however, it
would be more constructive to do so (because, I assume if they could do
this, they would be doing other constructive things as well, and
waitaminute, they would be eligible for rights, wouldn't they? But I
digress.) This is why I assume any superintelligence to come along
wouldn't simply bulldoze over us without a second thought, but would
respect what stunted reasoning capacity and intelligence we had, if only
out of recognition for our potential. Intelligence is not an entirely
relative measurement. There *is* point zero to measure from.

F.E.S.:
> [...] The F. E. S. submits
> that anything we speculate upon or discuss here is unlikely to weigh
> heavily with an advanced exploiter, of whatever origin.  
> [...] he has high statistical confidence in
> his ultimate doom at the appendage-equivalents of whatever the most
> successful exploiter of resources in the cosmos turns out to be.
> 
> Perhaps his only hope is that it will have chosen to be
> considerate of animals.

Could this, perchance, be at the root of the F.E.S.'s argument? It's
reasonable that from "Do unto others as you would have them do unto you"
follows "treat lesser creatures well, you'd want greater creatures to
treat you well." This is an understandable conclusion, but I find three
problems: 
*Fundamental Disagreement Alert* 
1) (as F.E.S. points out at beginning of quote) What's the guarantee
that greater intelligences will treat us well, even if we treat
squirrels well?
2) As I said above, intelligence is not an entirely relative scale.
Point 0, or the level of a rock, can be measured from, whether to
squirrels, humans, or superintelligences. We would not be squirrels to
superintelligences; we would be capable of reason, as a squirrel is not,
just not very bright in comparison to them.
3) (this follows partly from #2) There is a definite cutoff point
somewhere between squirrel and human. Somewhere, reason kicks in. Below
that line, discussion of and consideration for rights is meaningless.
Above that line, it is eminently meaningful. There isn't really a
continuum (probably because there aren't any intermediate beings. The
jump from primate to human is too extreme.) The way most people treat
dogs and cats differently from snails demonstrates, to a certain degree,
respect for different degrees of intelligence. Why shouldn't a
superintelligence respect that level in us? If they didn't, they
wouldn't be all that smart.

--Ratha, the philosophical perfectionist & nitpicker extraorinaire

