Date: Wed, 8 Sep 93 2:25:43 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: The Idea of Free Speech is Slipping Away

Extropiates,

Watching the debate on "animal rights" and plucky squirrels, I have
little to add and feel I've come in (after my vacation) about where I
came in last summer, when the debate about "the natural rights of
Martians" was just winding down. (Harry S. told me at the Extropolooza
that the idea of an archive of past postings is being again
considered. This is long overdue. Submission of posts to such an
archive would be voluntary, of course.)

I ventured my "calculus of valuing lives" and admitted I value certain
vanishng species more than I value many human lives. (But I agree with
Perry M. that "rights" is a misguided idea. I value tigers over most
human strangers, for example, for reasons that include esthetics, biodiversity,
and just because there are very few tigers and an awful lot of humans
living in squalor. I don't argue that tigers have "rights," only that
I will probably spend more per tiger to save the existing population
than I will to save most humans.) In any case, this view generated no
discussion, so I'll let it drop.

Free Speech

At the recent Worldcon in San Francisco, I got some insights into how
educated, SF-reading folks are gradually abandoning the two basic
tenets of free speech:

* "Sir, I disagree with everything you say, but I will defend to the
death your right to say it." (who said this? Voltaire?)

* "Sticks and stones may break my bones, but names can never hurt me."

These tenets, from diverse sources, neatly summarize what most of us
on this list think of as the basis of the "right" of free speech. (In
market terms, most of us realize names and speech is not agression,
and it is easier to just not try to limit what others say or write. We
mostly agree to just let folks say and think what they want. This is
the "social contract" which several Western nations were founded upon,
mostly, and it has worked pretty well. In terms of PPL/market law, it
is likely that free speech would also emerge, as few people would
choose to pay the added enforcement costs of trying to stop offensive
or tasteless blathering.)

But many educated Americans are increasingly calling for "speech
codes," for the outlawing of "hateful" and "hurtful" speech, and for
increased use of civil remedies (slander, defamation, damage) to
control the words of those they want silenced.

I mention Worldcon because in a panel I was on, on "Ethics and
Computers," a substantial fraction (though still a minority, I sensed)
of the audience was arguing for these speech codes, and for laws
designed to stop "agression" by male computer users and other unsavory
characters who make women on the nets uncomfortable, nervous, or just
plain angry. Several women stood up and spoke of the need for laws to
protect the "vulnerable" from "verbal assault."

Needless to say, I urged them not to throw away basic values of free
speech because of a few crude jokes, because some women feel the male
debating style is overly hormone-charged, or because some minority
members dislike some jokes told by some people. Other members of the
audience, including several Extropians, added their own support for
free speech.

"MUDs," the interactive systems based originally on "multi-user
dungeons" (as in D & D, one presumes), were singled out as an area
where women need the protection of the law to keep certain male (or
female?) users from harassing them or otherwise making their life less
than idyllic.

When some of us (especially Keith Henson) pointed out the importance
of _positive reputations_ in screening who one deals with, this was
rejected out of hand. Apparently these folks think computer networks
are a "commons" and invitation-only systems are unworkable. (I cited
crypto, of course, and mailing lists, positive reputation filters, kill
files, etc., as things that can be used to create "private spaces" in
these cypberspaces.)

A lawyer on the panel argued for new laws designed to protect users,
students, workers against "harassment." She argued that women and
other classes are exploited by free speech codes, that the free speech
is used by the oppressors to their own ends. (This line of reasoning
is not new: the idea that free society rights--free speech, freedom to
seek employment as one wishes, property rights, etc.--are "tools of
the oppressor class" is prevalent in many PC debates and on nearly
every college campus.)

I threw her for a loop when I pointed out that laws to control the
Internet and what is said on it, in newsgroups or in MUDs, are likely
to be fruitless: the Internet is transnational, is essentially
anarchic, and no country can control it. 

Lots more was said, and it was a pretty good debate, I think. I even
gave them a glimpse of a world of crypto anarchy!

I mention this to Extropians because it was fairly surprising to me to
see just how far the basic idea of free speech has been eroded, how
educated and intelligent folks are ready to abandon the ideas of free
speech and free association for the (putative) gains of having some
people sheltered from hearing things they don't want to hear.

These PC humanists speak of "tolerance" and how we need to be more
tolerant (multiculturalism, ethnic diversity, affirmative action,
etc.) while not realizing how intolerant this politically correct
agenda has become.

I'm losing my last remaining shreds of belief that the "masses" will
choose liberty. They won't.

We'll have to create our own libertarian and extropian future. Just as
we expected.

--Tim May

